Quoting  Derek Ray  <moot@just_a_spamtrap_anyway.com>:
>Who's acting?  The methods you listed of inventory destruction _ARE_ the
>result of player mistakes, no matter what else you want to call them.

On the other hand, there is something peculiarly harsh about the situation
where you cannot win the game but nothing particularly is being done about
it - where your character can roam the dungeon freely but has nothing to
do but escape it. Pre-Quest conversion has that characteristic, which is
why it's so unpopular; and so would the situation outlined.

Now I seriously doubt that Spork is ever going to reach a state where it
will say "no more monsters for you", so I don't think it's an issue, but I
greatly prefer game designs where you aren't just stopped. If your Crawl
character has no realistic chance of surviving Zot you are still permitted
to go on a banzai charge for the Orb.

I think this is why "if converted pre-Quest, allow the player to do this
very difficult thing" designs are so popular. If that thing was actually
very difficult I would regard it as improving NetHack.
